    "# 4.1 Data loading and initial data overview\n",
    "The dataset is loaded with pandas' read_excel() and inspected using head(), tail(), info() and describe(). head() and tail() confirm the general shape and formatting of the raw data by sampling its first and last records. info() reports the data type and non-null count of every column in a single call, which is the fastest way to spot columns needing type conversion or missing-value treatment. describe() is used specifically — rather than computing statistics manually — because it returns count, mean, standard deviation, minimum, quartiles and maximum for every numeric column in one comparable table, making it quick to notice extreme ranges or skew before any cleaning takes place. nunique() is also used to count distinct banks, categories and months, confirming the scope of the bank × month panel before deeper analysis begins.\n",
    "A quick visual check of missingness was added to complement the printed null counts, since a chart makes it easier to see at a glance which columns are affected and how their missingness compares in scale. This missing-value bar chart was added directly to the notebook as it did not previously exist as a visual check; null values were found only in atms_crms_onsite (7 rows), atms_crms_offsite (16 rows), and eight credit-card transaction columns (2 rows each).\n"
   ]
